WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.090
In this video, you add CPUs and memory to a running virtual machine in SUSE Virtualization, without restarting it, by using CPU and memory hotplug.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:17.540
The virtual machine demo-hotplug-cpu is running with one CPU and two gigabytes of memory.

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:20.370
Open its actions menu,

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:23.290
and select Edit Config.

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:33.270
CPU and memory hotplug was enabled when the virtual machine was created. It can grow up to four CPUs and eight gigabytes.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:36.030
Raise the CPU count to two,

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:38.880
and the memory to four gigabytes.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:47.960
The form also raised the maximums. Set Maximum CPU back to four, because changing a maximum requires a restart.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:51.460
Set Maximum Memory back to eight gigabytes.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:53.960
Select Save.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:01:00.100
The dialog offers a restart. Hotplug does not need one, so select Save.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:08.980
SUSE Virtualization applies the new CPU and memory with a live migration to another node.

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:18.200
The virtual machine kept running. It now has two CPUs and four gigabytes of memory, on a different node.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:25.820
The virtual machine now has two CPUs and four gigabytes of memory, and it never restarted. Next, you hot-plug a volume.
